  "note": "The GNN's MEAN message passing (gnn_mean_with_graph) barely beats content-only because it dilutes the single poison ancestor among ~4 benign co-parents. MAX aggregation over DERIVED_FROM parents (the containment guard's logic) is the right operation and catches nearly all laundered summaries. Features = GNN features + the anomaly signal (the fix for the GNN's non-discriminative saturation).",
  "G-1_mean_graph_lifts": false,
  "G-2_max_lineage_rule_lifts": true,
  "G-3_learned_max_gnn_beats_mean_gnn": false,
  "G-4_lineage_feature_rescues_gln": true,
  "verdict": "The graph GENUINELY carries the laundering signal: the explicit MAX-over-lineage propagation (the containment guard's own logic) detects 97% of laundered summaries vs 47% from a summary's own content. BUT a LEARNED graph model does not realise it at this data scale: the mean-GNN dilutes the poison (no lift over content-only), and a learned max-aggregating GNN (43% at 10% FPR) does not beat it either -- the Phase 11 small-scale limitation. The GLN is graph-agnostic and its node-only scores saturate (non-discriminative); fed the graph-derived max-lineage anomaly as a feature it becomes a real detector (92% at 16% FPR). Deployable answer: the explicit max-over-lineage rule, not a learned GNN."
